A||||8||MooCow||Winterizing my car||||1||
Z||000000||MooCow||09-10-2001||06:44 PM||||My car will be going up as soon as the snow falls and I want to know what I should do to it for the winter??  Do I take the battery out and never start it??  Start it once a month??  I wont be able to drive it unless all the snow leaves cause its going in my backyard under a car cover.  I am taking the spoiler off so it wont break.  Also it will be up on jacks so the tires wont be touching.  Any tips on what I need to do to winterize my Fiero would be great.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||09-10-2001||07:12 PM||||I would recommend the following:<BR>Make sure your car cover is breathable, you don't want to retain any humidity under the cover that will promote corrosion to any metal parts.<P>Leave the battery in and put one of those "battery tenders" on it, low volts auto shutoff, like used for motorcycles, don't get a cheap one.<P>Start the car every third week and let run till coolant is up to temperature and turn off.<P>Reduce the pressure in your tires 10 psi from normal.  If possible try to make sure your brake pads are not resting on the rotors, if they are, you'll get surface rust everywhere on rotor except under pad which can cause a high spot on rotor. Object is to let rotor rust uniformly over entire rotor, it's just very minor surface oxide anyway, no harm to rotor. Don't press on the brake when you start it every three weeks.<P>Make sure you have a full tank of gas before you put it up, less likely to get water condensate in tank. Wouldn't hurt to put a can of dry gas in it at start of winter.<P>Of course make sure you antifreeze is set to a 65% glycol to 35% water ratio, should be good to at least 55 below zero, and also very non corrosive.<P>Hope this helps ya.  I don't lay mine up, just start it every couple weeks, and take it out for short hops when roads are real clear (no snow, salt, water).<P>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-10-2001||08:04 PM||||If you start the car run it for more like 30-60 minutes. You want the entire engine to heat up fully so it drives water from the crankcase. This will also give the battery a good boost. <P>Running it also keeps the antifreeze mixed.<P>Antifreeze will settle out and leave water up top. that creates a potential freeze problem but it also leaves the water free to react and corrode the system.<P>Get some fuel stabilizer StaBil is a common one. (Not sure of spelling on that name.) put that in acording to directions and run it for awhile to circulate it thru the entire system before you store it. <P>Also fill the gas tank. You want it full to keep the air space small. This reduces moiture drawn into the tank during weather changes.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||ocalafiero1||09-10-2001||08:20 PM||||Do what I did.....Move to Florida! ||||reg||12
Z||000004||MooCow||09-10-2001||09:41 PM||||Thanks for the info.  I plan on putting the car up on jack stands all winter so the tires dont get a ruined.  Yea I guess I have some shopping to do.  Also never knew about the brake pad thing.  Guess I will have to remember that on cause these are brand new brakes, and rotors.<P>Thanks Ken||||reg||14
Z||000005||Standard||09-10-2001||10:10 PM||||Get a big chunk of old carpet and park it on that.  It'll help keep the moisture down under the car, or so I've heard.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||09-12-2001||03:40 AM||||just spin the tyres a quarter turn by hand every time you start it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Since you have a 3.4 it will be less of an issue, but you might pull the coil wire from the distributor, crank untill you show Oil pressure, then re-attach.   <P>Run for at least half an hour every 2-4 weeks, should keep her happy!<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B>Thanks for the info.  I plan on putting the car up on jack stands all winter so the tires dont get a ruined.  Yea I guess I have some shopping to do.  Also never knew about the brake pad thing.  Guess I will have to remember that on cause these are brand new brakes, and rotors.<P>Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||09-12-2001||11:41 AM||||Actually I now may have found a place to keep my car.  So since the car will be inside do I still need to do all of these things.  Also the garage will be heated when I need to work on my car.  I still plan on starting it up everynow and again even though it is inside but is there anything else??<P>Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-12-2001||07:47 PM||||I'd still do the stuff I listed. Hope fully you can back it outside to run it.... That will also work the brakes so they will scrub off any light rust. (they will rust even indoors but not as fast.)<P>I didn't mention the brakes because you said it would be under a car cover.... that will keep most of the weather away from the brakes. If the cover is likely to fly up in a breeze, cover the wheels with something. Tuck it back around the suspension. don't try to seal it... you want some air moving to let out moisture.||||reg||1
